1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,950
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:04,591
Investing has never been easier to start.

3
00:00:04,771 --> 00:00:06,501
You can open an account on your

4
00:00:06,501 --> 00:00:07,450
phone in minutes,

5
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:09,124
buy a fraction of a share with

6
00:00:09,124 --> 00:00:09,905
a few dollars,

7
00:00:09,905 --> 00:00:11,969
and trade without paying commissions.

8
00:00:12,149 --> 00:00:14,504
But easier to start doesn't mean easier

9
00:00:14,504 --> 00:00:15,168
to do well.

10
00:00:15,348 --> 00:00:17,622
In fact, it's never been easier to

11
00:00:17,622 --> 00:00:19,830
make expensive beginner mistakes.

12
00:00:20,010 --> 00:00:22,449
Today we're going through twenty of them,

13
00:00:22,449 --> 00:00:23,818
and what to do instead.

14
00:00:24,167 --> 00:00:25,445
Here's the good news.

15
00:00:25,625 --> 00:00:28,383
Most investing mistakes aren't about picking the

16
00:00:28,383 --> 00:00:29,073
wrong stock.

17
00:00:29,253 --> 00:00:31,281
They're about waiting too long,

18
00:00:31,281 --> 00:00:32,328
paying too much,

19
00:00:32,328 --> 00:00:35,534
reacting too emotionally and skipping the basics.

20
00:00:35,714 --> 00:00:37,291
And every one of those can be

21
00:00:37,291 --> 00:00:37,618
fixed.

22
00:00:37,967 --> 00:00:39,767
Think about it like learning to drive.

23
00:00:39,947 --> 00:00:42,843
Most crashes don't happen because someone lacked

24
00:00:42,843 --> 00:00:44,231
advanced racing skills.

25
00:00:44,411 --> 00:00:46,613
They happen because of basic mistakes,

26
00:00:46,613 --> 00:00:49,394
like speeding, not paying attention or panicking

27
00:00:49,394 --> 00:00:50,553
at the wrong moment.

28
00:00:50,733 --> 00:00:52,590
Investing works the same way.

29
00:00:52,770 --> 00:00:55,582
Avoiding the big basic mistakes matters far

30
00:00:55,582 --> 00:00:57,937
more than finding a clever strategy.

31
00:00:58,286 --> 00:01:00,620
We'll go through these from three angles.

32
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,286
First, the numbers.

33
00:01:02,466 --> 00:01:05,741
Mistakes that quietly cost you money through

34
00:01:05,741 --> 00:01:07,155
math, like waiting,

35
00:01:07,155 --> 00:01:08,271
fees and taxes.

36
00:01:08,451 --> 00:01:10,436
Second, the psychology.

37
00:01:10,616 --> 00:01:13,009
Mistakes our brains push us into,

38
00:01:13,009 --> 00:01:14,459
like panic and hype.

39
00:01:14,639 --> 00:01:16,137
And third, the playbook.

40
00:01:16,317 --> 00:01:18,274
Mistakes in how people set up and

41
00:01:18,274 --> 00:01:19,579
run their investments.

42
00:01:19,759 --> 00:01:22,465
Quick reminder, this is education,

43
00:01:22,465 --> 00:01:24,136
not financial advice,

44
00:01:24,136 --> 00:01:27,479
and all growth examples are illustrations,

45
00:01:27,479 --> 00:01:28,513
not promises.

46
00:01:28,863 --> 00:01:30,454
Let's start with the numbers.

47
00:01:30,634 --> 00:01:32,578
And the first mistake is the most

48
00:01:32,578 --> 00:01:33,815
expensive one of all.

49
00:01:34,165 --> 00:01:35,001
Our numbers.

50
00:01:35,181 --> 00:01:38,187
Growth examples use monthly compounding at a

51
00:01:38,187 --> 00:01:40,168
stated average yearly return,

52
00:01:40,168 --> 00:01:41,671
usually seven percent.

53
00:01:41,851 --> 00:01:44,540
Account and tax rules come from the

54
00:01:44,540 --> 00:01:44,847
IRS.

55
00:01:45,027 --> 00:01:47,740
Fund cost data comes from the Investment

56
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:51,065
Company Institute, and fund performance data from

57
00:01:51,065 --> 00:01:52,829
S and P Dow Jones Indices.

58
00:01:53,009 --> 00:01:55,816
Investor warnings come from the SEC and

59
00:01:55,816 --> 00:01:56,248
FINRA.

60
00:01:56,428 --> 00:01:57,837
This is education,

61
00:01:57,837 --> 00:01:59,481
not financial advice.

62
00:01:59,831 --> 00:02:00,528
Angle one.

63
00:02:00,708 --> 00:02:01,498
The numbers.

64
00:02:01,678 --> 00:02:04,959
Seven mistakes that quietly cost beginners money,

65
00:02:04,959 --> 00:02:07,169
often without them ever noticing.

66
00:02:07,519 --> 00:02:08,878
Mistake number one.

67
00:02:09,058 --> 00:02:10,846
Waiting for the right time to start.

68
00:02:11,195 --> 00:02:12,809
Beginners often wait.

69
00:02:12,989 --> 00:02:14,413
For a bigger paycheck,

70
00:02:14,413 --> 00:02:15,902
for the market to drop,

71
00:02:15,902 --> 00:02:17,132
for more knowledge,

72
00:02:17,132 --> 00:02:18,167
for less stress.

73
00:02:18,347 --> 00:02:21,248
But the most important ingredient in investing

74
00:02:21,248 --> 00:02:23,266
is time, and every year you wait

75
00:02:23,266 --> 00:02:25,284
is a year of compounding you can

76
00:02:25,284 --> 00:02:26,230
never get back.

77
00:02:26,410 --> 00:02:27,864
Waiting feels safe,

78
00:02:27,864 --> 00:02:30,925
but it's often the most expensive choice

79
00:02:30,925 --> 00:02:31,461
of all.

80
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:32,623
Here's the math.

81
00:02:32,803 --> 00:02:34,793
Two hundred dollars a month at a

82
00:02:34,793 --> 00:02:37,031
seven percent average yearly return,

83
00:02:37,031 --> 00:02:38,958
invested for thirty five years,

84
00:02:38,958 --> 00:02:41,756
grows to roughly three hundred sixty thousand

85
00:02:41,756 --> 00:02:42,254
dollars.

86
00:02:42,434 --> 00:02:44,771
Start ten years later and invest for

87
00:02:44,771 --> 00:02:45,940
twenty five years,

88
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,212
and it grows to roughly one hundred

89
00:02:48,212 --> 00:02:49,446
sixty two thousand.

90
00:02:49,626 --> 00:02:51,804
Waiting ten years cut the result by

91
00:02:51,804 --> 00:02:52,738
more than half.

92
00:02:53,087 --> 00:02:55,086
Waiting for the market to dip is

93
00:02:55,086 --> 00:02:57,209
a special version of this mistake.

94
00:02:57,389 --> 00:03:00,268
Nobody can reliably predict short term moves.

95
00:03:00,448 --> 00:03:02,707
People who wait for a crash sometimes

96
00:03:02,707 --> 00:03:05,026
watch the market rise for years before

97
00:03:05,026 --> 00:03:06,369
it comes, and by then,

98
00:03:06,369 --> 00:03:08,750
the crash may still leave prices higher

99
00:03:08,750 --> 00:03:10,642
than when they started waiting.

100
00:03:10,991 --> 00:03:12,292
What to do instead.

101
00:03:12,472 --> 00:03:14,552
Start now with whatever you can,

102
00:03:14,552 --> 00:03:16,828
even fifty or one hundred dollars a

103
00:03:16,828 --> 00:03:18,973
month, and increase it over time.

104
00:03:19,153 --> 00:03:21,200
You'll learn more from one year of

105
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:23,727
actually investing than from five years of

106
00:03:23,727 --> 00:03:25,051
waiting to feel ready.

107
00:03:25,401 --> 00:03:26,737
Mistake number two.

108
00:03:26,917 --> 00:03:28,356
Ignoring fees.

109
00:03:28,706 --> 00:03:31,326
Fees look tiny because they're shown as

110
00:03:31,326 --> 00:03:32,131
percentages.

111
00:03:32,311 --> 00:03:34,401
One percent a year sounds like nothing.

112
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:36,867
But that percentage comes out of your

113
00:03:36,867 --> 00:03:38,472
whole balance, every year,

114
00:03:38,472 --> 00:03:39,214
for decades.

115
00:03:39,394 --> 00:03:41,577
Beginners often don't even know what they're

116
00:03:41,577 --> 00:03:41,925
paying.

117
00:03:42,274 --> 00:03:45,448
According to the Investment Company Institute,

118
00:03:45,448 --> 00:03:48,552
the average index equity mutual fund recently

119
00:03:48,552 --> 00:03:51,519
charged around five hundredths of a percent

120
00:03:51,519 --> 00:03:54,417
a year, while the average actively managed

121
00:03:54,417 --> 00:03:57,797
equity fund charged around sixty five hundredths.

122
00:03:57,977 --> 00:04:00,367
Over forty years of steady investing,

123
00:04:00,367 --> 00:04:02,499
a gap like that can cost hundreds

124
00:04:02,499 --> 00:04:04,049
of thousands of dollars.

125
00:04:04,398 --> 00:04:06,337
And fund fees aren't the only ones.

126
00:04:06,517 --> 00:04:09,345
Some advisors charge around one percent a

127
00:04:09,345 --> 00:04:11,068
year on top of fund fees.

128
00:04:11,248 --> 00:04:13,536
Some accounts charge maintenance fees.

129
00:04:13,716 --> 00:04:16,445
Some products have sales charges when you

130
00:04:16,445 --> 00:04:16,711
buy.

131
00:04:16,891 --> 00:04:19,271
Fees stack, and they compound against you.

132
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:21,583
Here's a quick way to see fees

133
00:04:21,583 --> 00:04:22,302
in dollars.

134
00:04:22,482 --> 00:04:25,582
Multiply your balance by the expense ratio.

135
00:04:25,762 --> 00:04:27,818
On a fifty thousand dollar balance,

136
00:04:27,818 --> 00:04:29,757
a one percent fee is five hundred

137
00:04:29,757 --> 00:04:30,638
dollars a year.

138
00:04:30,818 --> 00:04:33,090
A five hundredths of a percent fee

139
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:34,626
is twenty five dollars.

140
00:04:34,806 --> 00:04:37,118
Same market exposure in many cases,

141
00:04:37,118 --> 00:04:38,439
very different cost,

142
00:04:38,439 --> 00:04:41,610
and that difference compounds every single year.

143
00:04:41,959 --> 00:04:43,364
What to do instead.

144
00:04:43,544 --> 00:04:45,850
Find the expense ratio of every fund

145
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:47,964
you own, and know exactly how any

146
00:04:47,964 --> 00:04:48,989
advisor is paid.

147
00:04:49,169 --> 00:04:51,652
Favor broad, low cost index funds,

148
00:04:51,652 --> 00:04:54,355
and treat every fee as something that

149
00:04:54,355 --> 00:04:55,961
has to justify itself.

150
00:04:56,311 --> 00:04:57,751
Mistake number three.

151
00:04:57,931 --> 00:04:59,684
Skipping the employer match.

152
00:05:00,034 --> 00:05:02,405
Many employers match part of what you

153
00:05:02,405 --> 00:05:05,096
contribute to a workplace retirement plan.

154
00:05:05,276 --> 00:05:07,558
A common example is fifty cents for

155
00:05:07,558 --> 00:05:09,383
every dollar you contribute,

156
00:05:09,383 --> 00:05:11,534
up to six percent of your salary.

157
00:05:11,714 --> 00:05:13,630
Not contributing enough to get the full

158
00:05:13,630 --> 00:05:15,301
match is like turning down part of

159
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:15,743
your pay.

160
00:05:16,092 --> 00:05:17,335
Here's what that can mean.

161
00:05:17,515 --> 00:05:19,654
On a sixty thousand dollar salary,

162
00:05:19,654 --> 00:05:22,548
contributing six percent is three thousand six

163
00:05:22,548 --> 00:05:23,995
hundred dollars a year,

164
00:05:23,995 --> 00:05:26,134
and a fifty percent match adds one

165
00:05:26,134 --> 00:05:27,581
thousand eight hundred.

166
00:05:27,761 --> 00:05:29,916
Invested for thirty years at a seven

167
00:05:29,916 --> 00:05:31,712
percent average yearly return,

168
00:05:31,712 --> 00:05:33,988
that match alone could grow to roughly

169
00:05:33,988 --> 00:05:36,143
one hundred eighty thousand dollars.

170
00:05:36,492 --> 00:05:39,092
Some matches come with a vesting schedule,

171
00:05:39,092 --> 00:05:41,196
meaning you have to stay a certain

172
00:05:41,196 --> 00:05:43,053
number of years to keep all of

173
00:05:43,053 --> 00:05:44,352
the employer's money.

174
00:05:44,532 --> 00:05:46,017
Even so, for most people,

175
00:05:46,017 --> 00:05:47,857
getting the match is one of the

176
00:05:47,857 --> 00:05:49,757
best returns available anywhere.

177
00:05:50,106 --> 00:05:51,476
What to do instead.

178
00:05:51,656 --> 00:05:53,926
Find your plan's match formula in your

179
00:05:53,926 --> 00:05:56,913
benefits information, and set your contribution at

180
00:05:56,913 --> 00:05:59,123
least high enough to get every dollar

181
00:05:59,123 --> 00:05:59,482
of it.

182
00:05:59,831 --> 00:06:01,167
Mistake number four.

183
00:06:01,347 --> 00:06:03,901
Investing while carrying high interest debt.

184
00:06:04,250 --> 00:06:07,302
Investing feels more exciting than paying off

185
00:06:07,302 --> 00:06:07,641
debt.

186
00:06:07,821 --> 00:06:09,984
But credit card interest rates in the

187
00:06:09,984 --> 00:06:12,791
United States have averaged above twenty percent

188
00:06:12,791 --> 00:06:13,727
in recent years,

189
00:06:13,727 --> 00:06:15,715
according to Federal Reserve data.

190
00:06:15,895 --> 00:06:18,840
Long term stock market returns have historically

191
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,435
been much lower than that.

192
00:06:20,784 --> 00:06:22,853
So if you invest while carrying a

193
00:06:22,853 --> 00:06:24,483
large credit card balance,

194
00:06:24,483 --> 00:06:26,427
you're usually losing the race.

195
00:06:26,607 --> 00:06:28,980
The debt grows faster than your investments

196
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:29,753
are likely to.

197
00:06:29,933 --> 00:06:32,122
Paying off a twenty percent card is

198
00:06:32,122 --> 00:06:35,123
like earning a guaranteed twenty percent return.

199
00:06:35,472 --> 00:06:38,201
The exception is usually the employer match.

200
00:06:38,381 --> 00:06:40,952
Many people still contribute enough to get

201
00:06:40,952 --> 00:06:42,544
it while paying down debt,

202
00:06:42,544 --> 00:06:44,503
because the match is such a high

203
00:06:44,503 --> 00:06:45,544
immediate return.

204
00:06:45,894 --> 00:06:47,357
What to do instead.

205
00:06:47,537 --> 00:06:49,418
List your debts by interest rate.

206
00:06:49,598 --> 00:06:52,210
Attack the highest rate first while making

207
00:06:52,210 --> 00:06:54,013
minimum payments on the rest,

208
00:06:54,013 --> 00:06:56,998
then redirect those payments into investing once

209
00:06:56,998 --> 00:06:58,677
the expensive debt is gone.

210
00:06:59,027 --> 00:07:00,223
Mistake number five.

211
00:07:00,403 --> 00:07:02,632
Investing without an emergency fund.

212
00:07:02,982 --> 00:07:04,062
Picture this.

213
00:07:04,242 --> 00:07:06,100
You invest everything you have.

214
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:07,754
Then the market drops,

215
00:07:07,754 --> 00:07:09,430
and around the same time,

216
00:07:09,430 --> 00:07:11,574
your car breaks down or you lose

217
00:07:11,574 --> 00:07:12,178
your job.

218
00:07:12,358 --> 00:07:14,641
Now you have to sell investments at

219
00:07:14,641 --> 00:07:15,945
a loss to pay bills.

220
00:07:16,125 --> 00:07:18,906
That's how beginners turn temporary drops into

221
00:07:18,906 --> 00:07:19,933
permanent losses.

222
00:07:20,283 --> 00:07:22,700
An emergency fund is cash set aside

223
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:25,462
for surprises, often three to six months

224
00:07:25,462 --> 00:07:26,981
of essential expenses,

225
00:07:26,981 --> 00:07:29,604
kept somewhere safe and easy to reach,

226
00:07:29,604 --> 00:07:31,952
like a high yield savings account.

227
00:07:32,132 --> 00:07:33,420
It's not an investment.

228
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,574
It's insurance for your investments.

229
00:07:35,923 --> 00:07:37,677
It also helps you stay calm.

230
00:07:37,857 --> 00:07:39,861
When you know your bills are covered,

231
00:07:39,861 --> 00:07:41,432
market drops feel less scary.

232
00:07:41,612 --> 00:07:43,899
You can leave your investments alone and

233
00:07:43,899 --> 00:07:44,870
let them recover,

234
00:07:44,870 --> 00:07:46,071
instead of panicking.

235
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:47,581
What to do instead.

236
00:07:47,761 --> 00:07:50,260
Build at least a starter emergency fund,

237
00:07:50,260 --> 00:07:51,947
maybe one thousand dollars,

238
00:07:51,947 --> 00:07:53,509
before investing heavily,

239
00:07:53,509 --> 00:07:55,508
then grow it toward three to six

240
00:07:55,508 --> 00:07:57,444
months of essentials over time.

241
00:07:57,795 --> 00:07:59,339
Mistake number six.

242
00:07:59,519 --> 00:08:01,295
Expecting smooth returns.

243
00:08:01,645 --> 00:08:04,283
Beginners often hear that the stock market

244
00:08:04,283 --> 00:08:06,670
has averaged around ten percent a year

245
00:08:06,670 --> 00:08:08,617
historically, before inflation,

246
00:08:08,617 --> 00:08:11,129
and expect something close to that every

247
00:08:11,129 --> 00:08:11,443
year.

248
00:08:11,623 --> 00:08:13,702
But averages hide huge swings.

249
00:08:13,882 --> 00:08:16,570
Individual years can be up thirty percent

250
00:08:16,570 --> 00:08:18,735
or down more than thirty percent.

251
00:08:19,085 --> 00:08:21,261
The S and P five hundred fell

252
00:08:21,261 --> 00:08:23,438
by more than a third in 2008.

253
00:08:23,618 --> 00:08:26,204
It dropped sharply in early 2020,

254
00:08:26,204 --> 00:08:28,007
then recovered quickly.

255
00:08:28,187 --> 00:08:30,828
In 2022 it fell by around a

256
00:08:30,828 --> 00:08:31,414
fifth.

257
00:08:31,594 --> 00:08:34,509
Long term investors have been rewarded historically,

258
00:08:34,509 --> 00:08:36,471
but the path has never been smooth.

259
00:08:36,821 --> 00:08:38,492
Here's another quirk of the math.

260
00:08:38,672 --> 00:08:40,900
If your investment falls thirty percent,

261
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:42,570
you need a gain of about forty

262
00:08:42,570 --> 00:08:44,408
three percent just to get back to

263
00:08:44,408 --> 00:08:44,686
even.

264
00:08:44,866 --> 00:08:47,344
Losses and gains aren't symmetrical,

265
00:08:47,344 --> 00:08:49,614
which is one more reason to avoid

266
00:08:49,614 --> 00:08:51,472
panic selling after a drop.

267
00:08:51,823 --> 00:08:53,847
It also helps to know how often

268
00:08:53,847 --> 00:08:54,957
bad years happen.

269
00:08:55,137 --> 00:08:57,580
Historically, the S and P five hundred

270
00:08:57,580 --> 00:09:00,151
has had a negative calendar year roughly

271
00:09:00,151 --> 00:09:01,244
one year in four,

272
00:09:01,244 --> 00:09:02,080
give or take.

273
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:04,854
That means a forty year investing life

274
00:09:04,854 --> 00:09:07,380
could include about ten losing years.

275
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:09,309
They are not a sign that something

276
00:09:09,309 --> 00:09:09,824
is broken.

277
00:09:10,004 --> 00:09:11,786
They are the price of admission for

278
00:09:11,786 --> 00:09:12,651
long term growth.

279
00:09:13,001 --> 00:09:14,394
What to do instead.

280
00:09:14,574 --> 00:09:16,060
Plan for bad years.

281
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:19,124
Expect them, write down how you'll respond,

282
00:09:19,124 --> 00:09:21,539
and only invest money in stocks that

283
00:09:21,539 --> 00:09:23,686
you won't need for at least five

284
00:09:23,686 --> 00:09:24,088
years.

285
00:09:24,439 --> 00:09:25,855
Mistake number seven.

286
00:09:26,035 --> 00:09:27,417
Ignoring taxes.

287
00:09:27,767 --> 00:09:29,658
Taxes can take a big bite out

288
00:09:29,658 --> 00:09:32,527
of returns, but beginners often ignore them.

289
00:09:32,707 --> 00:09:35,588
One common mistake is investing in a

290
00:09:35,588 --> 00:09:39,830
regular taxable account while ignoring tax advantaged

291
00:09:39,830 --> 00:09:42,231
accounts like a four oh one k,

292
00:09:42,231 --> 00:09:44,793
a traditional IRA or a Roth IRA.

293
00:09:45,143 --> 00:09:46,954
Another is selling too quickly.

294
00:09:47,134 --> 00:09:48,540
In the United States,

295
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:51,153
profits on investments held one year or

296
00:09:51,153 --> 00:09:54,101
less are generally taxed as ordinary income,

297
00:09:54,101 --> 00:09:56,847
while investments held longer than a year

298
00:09:56,847 --> 00:09:59,728
usually qualify for lower long term capital

299
00:09:59,728 --> 00:10:00,532
gains rates.

300
00:10:00,712 --> 00:10:03,475
Frequent trading can mean paying more tax.

301
00:10:03,825 --> 00:10:06,412
Each tax advantaged account has rules,

302
00:10:06,412 --> 00:10:09,749
income limits and yearly contribution limits that

303
00:10:09,749 --> 00:10:10,907
change over time.

304
00:10:11,087 --> 00:10:13,966
The IRS website lists the current numbers.

305
00:10:14,316 --> 00:10:15,593
What to do instead.

306
00:10:15,773 --> 00:10:18,710
Use tax advantaged accounts first where it

307
00:10:18,710 --> 00:10:21,648
makes sense, hold investments for the long

308
00:10:21,648 --> 00:10:24,097
term, and consider talking to a tax

309
00:10:24,097 --> 00:10:27,034
professional if your situation is complex.

310
00:10:27,385 --> 00:10:28,313
That's angle one.

311
00:10:28,493 --> 00:10:31,214
Waiting, fees, skipping the match,

312
00:10:31,214 --> 00:10:33,935
expensive debt, no emergency fund,

313
00:10:33,935 --> 00:10:37,456
expecting smooth returns and ignoring taxes.

314
00:10:37,636 --> 00:10:38,879
Now angle two.

315
00:10:39,059 --> 00:10:40,173
The psychology.

316
00:10:40,523 --> 00:10:43,107
Most investing mistakes aren't caused by a

317
00:10:43,107 --> 00:10:44,215
lack of knowledge.

318
00:10:44,395 --> 00:10:46,078
They're caused by emotions.

319
00:10:46,258 --> 00:10:48,756
Fear, greed, excitement,

320
00:10:48,756 --> 00:10:49,277
envy.

321
00:10:49,457 --> 00:10:50,353
In this angle,

322
00:10:50,353 --> 00:10:52,849
we'll look at seven mistakes our brains

323
00:10:52,849 --> 00:10:53,681
push us into,

324
00:10:53,681 --> 00:10:56,049
and how to protect yourself from your

325
00:10:56,049 --> 00:10:56,945
own instincts.

326
00:10:57,295 --> 00:10:58,653
Mistake number eight.

327
00:10:58,833 --> 00:11:00,969
Panic selling during a crash.

328
00:11:01,320 --> 00:11:02,558
When markets fall,

329
00:11:02,558 --> 00:11:04,829
it feels like the drop will never

330
00:11:04,829 --> 00:11:05,104
end.

331
00:11:05,284 --> 00:11:06,412
The news is scary,

332
00:11:06,412 --> 00:11:09,168
your balance is shrinking and every instinct

333
00:11:09,168 --> 00:11:11,298
says get out before it gets worse.

334
00:11:11,478 --> 00:11:13,247
So many beginners sell,

335
00:11:13,247 --> 00:11:16,093
turning a temporary paper loss into a

336
00:11:16,093 --> 00:11:17,632
real, permanent one.

337
00:11:17,982 --> 00:11:19,792
The problem is that some of the

338
00:11:19,792 --> 00:11:22,538
market's best days have historically come close

339
00:11:22,538 --> 00:11:23,589
to its worst ones.

340
00:11:23,769 --> 00:11:25,920
Investors who sell in a panic often

341
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:26,965
miss the rebound,

342
00:11:26,965 --> 00:11:28,748
and then buy back in later at

343
00:11:28,748 --> 00:11:30,838
higher prices, if they buy back at

344
00:11:30,838 --> 00:11:31,083
all.

345
00:11:31,434 --> 00:11:33,314
Historically, the broad U.

346
00:11:33,494 --> 00:11:33,901
S.

347
00:11:34,081 --> 00:11:36,700
market has recovered from every past crash,

348
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:39,562
although some recoveries took years and there's

349
00:11:39,562 --> 00:11:41,267
no guarantee for the future.

350
00:11:41,447 --> 00:11:43,766
The investors who stayed in through those

351
00:11:43,766 --> 00:11:45,859
drops were usually the ones rewarded.

352
00:11:46,209 --> 00:11:48,469
There's also a hidden cost to panic

353
00:11:48,469 --> 00:11:50,342
selling in a taxable account.

354
00:11:50,522 --> 00:11:53,067
If you sell investments that had gains,

355
00:11:53,067 --> 00:11:54,242
you may owe taxes,

356
00:11:54,242 --> 00:11:55,939
and if you buy back later,

357
00:11:55,939 --> 00:11:57,897
you start over with a new cost

358
00:11:57,897 --> 00:11:58,289
basis.

359
00:11:58,469 --> 00:12:00,523
So panic can cost you twice,

360
00:12:00,523 --> 00:12:03,091
once in missed recovery and once in

361
00:12:03,091 --> 00:12:03,531
taxes.

362
00:12:03,711 --> 00:12:05,545
Staying invested avoids both.

363
00:12:05,895 --> 00:12:07,195
What to do instead.

364
00:12:07,375 --> 00:12:09,397
Decide now how you'll act in a

365
00:12:09,397 --> 00:12:09,801
crash.

366
00:12:09,981 --> 00:12:10,991
Write it down.

367
00:12:11,171 --> 00:12:12,272
When a crash comes,

368
00:12:12,272 --> 00:12:15,112
keep your automatic investments running and avoid

369
00:12:15,112 --> 00:12:17,487
making big decisions while you're scared.

370
00:12:17,838 --> 00:12:19,312
Mistake number nine.

371
00:12:19,492 --> 00:12:21,292
Chasing last year's winners.

372
00:12:21,642 --> 00:12:23,924
Beginners often look at which fund or

373
00:12:23,924 --> 00:12:25,897
stock went up the most last year

374
00:12:25,897 --> 00:12:26,576
and buy it.

375
00:12:26,756 --> 00:12:28,462
It feels logical.

376
00:12:28,642 --> 00:12:29,894
If it went up a lot,

377
00:12:29,894 --> 00:12:30,895
it must be good.

378
00:12:31,075 --> 00:12:33,699
But past performance often doesn't continue.

379
00:12:34,049 --> 00:12:36,369
S and P Dow Jones Indices tracks

380
00:12:36,369 --> 00:12:39,343
whether top performing funds stay on top.

381
00:12:39,523 --> 00:12:42,669
Their persistence research has found that relatively

382
00:12:42,669 --> 00:12:44,848
few top funds keep their top ranking

383
00:12:44,848 --> 00:12:46,361
over the following years.

384
00:12:46,541 --> 00:12:49,747
Yesterday's winner is often tomorrow's average,

385
00:12:49,747 --> 00:12:50,361
or worse.

386
00:12:50,711 --> 00:12:54,057
Chasing performance also means buying after prices

387
00:12:54,057 --> 00:12:55,730
have already risen a lot,

388
00:12:55,730 --> 00:12:57,537
which can mean buying high.

389
00:12:57,717 --> 00:12:59,708
Then, when the fund cools off,

390
00:12:59,708 --> 00:13:01,832
many people sell low and move to

391
00:13:01,832 --> 00:13:03,093
the next hot thing.

392
00:13:03,443 --> 00:13:05,061
Fund companies know this,

393
00:13:05,061 --> 00:13:07,975
which is why advertisements often highlight a

394
00:13:07,975 --> 00:13:09,724
fund's best recent returns.

395
00:13:09,904 --> 00:13:12,105
A fund family with dozens of funds

396
00:13:12,105 --> 00:13:14,306
will almost always have a few with

397
00:13:14,306 --> 00:13:15,666
great recent numbers,

398
00:13:15,666 --> 00:13:16,637
just by chance.

399
00:13:16,817 --> 00:13:18,890
Seeing a strong track record in an

400
00:13:18,890 --> 00:13:21,023
ad tells you very little about what

401
00:13:21,023 --> 00:13:22,425
that fund will do next.

402
00:13:22,775 --> 00:13:24,063
What to do instead.

403
00:13:24,243 --> 00:13:26,713
Choose investments based on cost,

404
00:13:26,713 --> 00:13:28,959
diversification and your plan,

405
00:13:28,959 --> 00:13:30,606
not on recent returns.

406
00:13:30,786 --> 00:13:33,485
Broad, low cost index funds remove the

407
00:13:33,485 --> 00:13:35,615
need to guess who'll win next.

408
00:13:35,966 --> 00:13:37,370
Mistake number ten.

409
00:13:37,550 --> 00:13:38,619
Falling for hype.

410
00:13:38,969 --> 00:13:39,970
Every few years,

411
00:13:39,970 --> 00:13:43,601
something becomes the investment everyone's talking about.

412
00:13:43,781 --> 00:13:44,870
A hot sector,

413
00:13:44,870 --> 00:13:45,959
a meme stock,

414
00:13:45,959 --> 00:13:46,881
a new coin.

415
00:13:47,061 --> 00:13:50,113
Prices soar, people brag online and beginners

416
00:13:50,113 --> 00:13:52,216
rush in so they don't miss out.

417
00:13:52,566 --> 00:13:55,180
Some hyped investments keep rising for a

418
00:13:55,180 --> 00:13:55,573
while.

419
00:13:55,753 --> 00:13:57,782
But many end with sharp crashes,

420
00:13:57,782 --> 00:13:59,747
and the people who buy in late,

421
00:13:59,747 --> 00:14:02,093
often beginners, tend to buy near the

422
00:14:02,093 --> 00:14:02,347
top.

423
00:14:02,527 --> 00:14:04,520
History is full of bubbles that felt

424
00:14:04,520 --> 00:14:06,126
like sure things at the time.

425
00:14:06,476 --> 00:14:09,138
The SEC and FINRA regularly warn investors

426
00:14:09,138 --> 00:14:10,913
about pump and dump schemes,

427
00:14:10,913 --> 00:14:13,575
where promoters hype an investment to push

428
00:14:13,575 --> 00:14:14,399
its price up,

429
00:14:14,399 --> 00:14:16,491
then sell their own shares to the

430
00:14:16,491 --> 00:14:18,329
people they convinced to buy.

431
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:20,322
A useful test is to ask how

432
00:14:20,322 --> 00:14:22,511
the investment actually makes money.

433
00:14:22,691 --> 00:14:24,374
A company earns profits.

434
00:14:24,554 --> 00:14:26,424
A bond pays interest.

435
00:14:26,604 --> 00:14:28,600
A rental property collects rent.

436
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:30,872
If the only reason something should go

437
00:14:30,872 --> 00:14:32,689
up is that other people will want

438
00:14:32,689 --> 00:14:34,175
to buy it later at a higher

439
00:14:34,175 --> 00:14:35,606
price, you're speculating,

440
00:14:35,606 --> 00:14:37,643
not investing, and you should size it

441
00:14:37,643 --> 00:14:38,138
that way.

442
00:14:38,488 --> 00:14:39,916
What to do instead.

443
00:14:40,096 --> 00:14:42,073
If you want to try a speculative

444
00:14:42,073 --> 00:14:44,484
investment, use only a small amount you

445
00:14:44,484 --> 00:14:46,338
can afford to lose completely,

446
00:14:46,338 --> 00:14:48,315
and never borrow money to do it.

447
00:14:48,666 --> 00:14:49,850
Quick pause.

448
00:14:50,030 --> 00:14:52,000
If this list is saving you from

449
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,716
even one of these mistakes,

450
00:14:53,716 --> 00:14:56,067
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

451
00:14:56,247 --> 00:14:58,055
It's free, and it helps us keep

452
00:14:58,055 --> 00:14:58,813
making these.

453
00:14:58,993 --> 00:15:00,653
Now, mistake eleven.

454
00:15:01,003 --> 00:15:02,384
Mistake number eleven.

455
00:15:02,564 --> 00:15:05,165
Getting overconfident after early wins.

456
00:15:05,515 --> 00:15:08,618
Many beginners start investing during a rising

457
00:15:08,618 --> 00:15:09,090
market.

458
00:15:09,270 --> 00:15:10,555
Their first picks go up,

459
00:15:10,555 --> 00:15:12,857
and they conclude they're naturally good at

460
00:15:12,857 --> 00:15:13,125
this.

461
00:15:13,305 --> 00:15:15,402
So they invest more aggressively,

462
00:15:15,402 --> 00:15:17,879
trade more often and take bigger risks.

463
00:15:18,230 --> 00:15:19,696
But in a rising market,

464
00:15:19,696 --> 00:15:21,353
almost everything goes up.

465
00:15:21,533 --> 00:15:23,574
Early gains often say more about the

466
00:15:23,574 --> 00:15:24,934
market than about skill.

467
00:15:25,114 --> 00:15:26,538
When the market turns,

468
00:15:26,538 --> 00:15:29,062
the bigger risks lead to bigger losses.

469
00:15:29,412 --> 00:15:31,954
Research by Brad Barber and Terrance Odean

470
00:15:31,954 --> 00:15:34,738
found that the individual investors who traded

471
00:15:34,738 --> 00:15:37,341
most often earned lower returns after costs

472
00:15:37,341 --> 00:15:39,036
than those who traded least.

473
00:15:39,216 --> 00:15:41,071
Confidence led to activity,

474
00:15:41,071 --> 00:15:43,268
and activity often hurt returns.

475
00:15:43,618 --> 00:15:45,080
What to do instead.

476
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:47,935
Compare your results with a simple index

477
00:15:47,935 --> 00:15:49,540
fund over several years,

478
00:15:49,540 --> 00:15:50,810
including bad ones.

479
00:15:50,990 --> 00:15:52,398
If you're not beating it,

480
00:15:52,398 --> 00:15:54,427
the index fund is the better choice.

481
00:15:54,777 --> 00:15:56,112
Mistake number twelve.

482
00:15:56,292 --> 00:15:58,207
Checking your investments too often.

483
00:15:58,558 --> 00:16:00,689
Investing apps make it easy to check

484
00:16:00,689 --> 00:16:02,760
your balance dozens of times a day.

485
00:16:02,940 --> 00:16:04,655
But the more often you look,

486
00:16:04,655 --> 00:16:06,493
the more often you see losses.

487
00:16:06,673 --> 00:16:07,999
Over short periods,

488
00:16:07,999 --> 00:16:09,954
markets go down quite often,

489
00:16:09,954 --> 00:16:12,257
even when they rise over the long

490
00:16:12,257 --> 00:16:12,536
run.

491
00:16:12,886 --> 00:16:16,903
Economists Shlomo Benartzi and Richard Thaler called

492
00:16:16,903 --> 00:16:18,911
this myopic loss aversion.

493
00:16:19,091 --> 00:16:21,894
Because losses feel worse than gains feel

494
00:16:21,894 --> 00:16:24,697
good, investors who check often feel more

495
00:16:24,697 --> 00:16:27,159
pain and tend to make more cautious,

496
00:16:27,159 --> 00:16:30,235
emotional decisions, which can hurt long term

497
00:16:30,235 --> 00:16:30,782
returns.

498
00:16:31,133 --> 00:16:33,783
Frequent checking also creates the urge to

499
00:16:33,783 --> 00:16:34,604
do something.

500
00:16:34,784 --> 00:16:36,523
And for long term investors,

501
00:16:36,523 --> 00:16:39,070
doing something is often worse than doing

502
00:16:39,070 --> 00:16:39,567
nothing.

503
00:16:39,918 --> 00:16:41,102
Think about it this way.

504
00:16:41,282 --> 00:16:42,814
If you owned a rental house,

505
00:16:42,814 --> 00:16:44,675
you wouldn't ask for a price quote

506
00:16:44,675 --> 00:16:46,481
every hour and panic when it came

507
00:16:46,481 --> 00:16:47,795
in lower than yesterday.

508
00:16:47,975 --> 00:16:49,345
You'd look at the rent,

509
00:16:49,345 --> 00:16:51,609
the condition and the long term value.

510
00:16:51,789 --> 00:16:54,138
Treat your index funds the same way,

511
00:16:54,138 --> 00:16:55,966
as long term assets you own,

512
00:16:55,966 --> 00:16:57,989
not as numbers on a scoreboard.

513
00:16:58,339 --> 00:16:59,732
What to do instead.

514
00:16:59,912 --> 00:17:01,785
Turn off price notifications,

515
00:17:01,785 --> 00:17:03,982
move the app off your home screen,

516
00:17:03,982 --> 00:17:06,695
and review your investments on a schedule,

517
00:17:06,695 --> 00:17:08,633
like once a quarter or twice a

518
00:17:08,633 --> 00:17:08,956
year.

519
00:17:09,306 --> 00:17:11,001
Mistake number thirteen.

520
00:17:11,181 --> 00:17:13,955
Taking investing advice from social media.

521
00:17:14,306 --> 00:17:17,250
Social media is full of people sharing

522
00:17:17,250 --> 00:17:21,046
stock tips, trading strategies and screenshots of

523
00:17:21,046 --> 00:17:21,898
huge gains.

524
00:17:22,078 --> 00:17:23,123
Some are honest.

525
00:17:23,303 --> 00:17:25,231
Some are paid to promote products.

526
00:17:25,411 --> 00:17:26,920
Some are simply lucky.

527
00:17:27,100 --> 00:17:29,030
And it's very hard to tell which

528
00:17:29,030 --> 00:17:29,573
is which.

529
00:17:29,923 --> 00:17:32,471
FINRA and the SEC have warned investors

530
00:17:32,471 --> 00:17:36,327
about financial influencers who promote investments without

531
00:17:36,327 --> 00:17:38,614
disclosing that they're being paid,

532
00:17:38,614 --> 00:17:41,359
and about scams that use fake testimonials

533
00:17:41,359 --> 00:17:42,601
and impersonations.

534
00:17:42,781 --> 00:17:45,990
If something promises guaranteed high returns with

535
00:17:45,990 --> 00:17:48,365
little risk, that's a major red flag.

536
00:17:48,715 --> 00:17:50,653
You also only see the winners.

537
00:17:50,833 --> 00:17:53,091
People rarely post their losses,

538
00:17:53,091 --> 00:17:55,984
so social media gives a distorted picture

539
00:17:55,984 --> 00:17:58,101
of how investing usually goes.

540
00:17:58,452 --> 00:18:00,716
Ask yourself how the person makes money.

541
00:18:00,896 --> 00:18:02,488
If they earn commissions,

542
00:18:02,488 --> 00:18:05,483
referral fees or sponsorships from the products

543
00:18:05,483 --> 00:18:07,967
they recommend, their advice may not be

544
00:18:07,967 --> 00:18:08,477
neutral.

545
00:18:08,657 --> 00:18:10,652
That doesn't make them dishonest,

546
00:18:10,652 --> 00:18:12,466
but it does mean you should be

547
00:18:12,466 --> 00:18:15,368
more skeptical, and look for independent sources

548
00:18:15,368 --> 00:18:16,215
before acting.

549
00:18:16,565 --> 00:18:17,911
What to do instead.

550
00:18:18,091 --> 00:18:21,016
Learn from sources with accountability,

551
00:18:21,016 --> 00:18:23,565
like books by established authors,

552
00:18:23,565 --> 00:18:26,640
official resources like Investor dot gov,

553
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:29,040
and fee only fiduciary advisors.

554
00:18:29,220 --> 00:18:31,813
And check any professional's background on FINRA's

555
00:18:31,813 --> 00:18:32,436
BrokerCheck.

556
00:18:32,786 --> 00:18:34,481
Mistake number fourteen.

557
00:18:34,661 --> 00:18:36,642
Putting too much in one company,

558
00:18:36,642 --> 00:18:38,190
especially your employer.

559
00:18:38,540 --> 00:18:40,747
When Enron collapsed in 2001,

560
00:18:40,747 --> 00:18:43,412
many employees had a large share of

561
00:18:43,412 --> 00:18:46,458
their retirement savings in Enron stock.

562
00:18:46,638 --> 00:18:48,309
They lost their jobs and much of

563
00:18:48,309 --> 00:18:50,399
their retirement money at the same time.

564
00:18:50,579 --> 00:18:52,764
It's one of the most painful lessons

565
00:18:52,764 --> 00:18:54,039
in investing history.

566
00:18:54,389 --> 00:18:56,545
Owning a lot of your employer's stock

567
00:18:56,545 --> 00:18:57,361
feels natural.

568
00:18:57,541 --> 00:18:58,633
You know the company,

569
00:18:58,633 --> 00:19:00,506
you believe in it and sometimes it's

570
00:19:00,506 --> 00:19:01,651
offered at a discount.

571
00:19:01,831 --> 00:19:04,016
But your paycheck already depends on that

572
00:19:04,016 --> 00:19:04,443
company.

573
00:19:04,623 --> 00:19:06,901
Putting your savings there too doubles your

574
00:19:06,901 --> 00:19:07,165
risk.

575
00:19:07,516 --> 00:19:09,605
The same goes for any single stock.

576
00:19:09,785 --> 00:19:11,819
Even great companies can stumble,

577
00:19:11,819 --> 00:19:14,653
and research by Hendrik Bessembinder has shown

578
00:19:14,653 --> 00:19:17,796
that most individual stocks have underperformed the

579
00:19:17,796 --> 00:19:20,014
overall market over their lifetimes.

580
00:19:20,364 --> 00:19:21,641
What to do instead.

581
00:19:21,821 --> 00:19:25,300
Many financial planners suggest keeping any single

582
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:27,596
stock, including your employer's,

583
00:19:27,596 --> 00:19:30,031
to a small share of your portfolio,

584
00:19:30,031 --> 00:19:31,840
often ten percent or less.

585
00:19:32,020 --> 00:19:34,248
Spread the rest across broad,

586
00:19:34,248 --> 00:19:35,631
diversified funds.

587
00:19:35,981 --> 00:19:37,014
That's angle two.

588
00:19:37,194 --> 00:19:39,716
Panic selling, chasing winners,

589
00:19:39,716 --> 00:19:43,052
hype, overconfidence, checking too often,

590
00:19:43,052 --> 00:19:45,900
social media advice and too much in

591
00:19:45,900 --> 00:19:46,877
one company.

592
00:19:47,057 --> 00:19:48,310
Now angle three.

593
00:19:48,490 --> 00:19:49,233
The playbook.

594
00:19:49,584 --> 00:19:51,562
The final six mistakes are about how

595
00:19:51,562 --> 00:19:53,926
people set up and manage their investments.

596
00:19:54,106 --> 00:19:56,205
These are often the easiest to fix,

597
00:19:56,205 --> 00:19:59,384
because they don't require changing your personality,

598
00:19:59,384 --> 00:20:00,944
just changing your system.

599
00:20:01,294 --> 00:20:02,977
Mistake number fifteen.

600
00:20:03,157 --> 00:20:04,899
Investing without a plan.

601
00:20:05,249 --> 00:20:07,768
Many beginners buy investments randomly.

602
00:20:07,948 --> 00:20:09,801
A stock a friend mentioned,

603
00:20:09,801 --> 00:20:11,380
a fund they saw online,

604
00:20:11,380 --> 00:20:13,439
something that seemed popular.

605
00:20:13,619 --> 00:20:14,523
Without a plan,

606
00:20:14,523 --> 00:20:16,992
every decision is based on whatever feels

607
00:20:16,992 --> 00:20:17,895
right that day,

608
00:20:17,895 --> 00:20:20,063
and feelings change with the market.

609
00:20:20,413 --> 00:20:22,851
A simple plan answers a few questions.

610
00:20:23,031 --> 00:20:24,366
What is this money for?

611
00:20:24,546 --> 00:20:25,579
When will I need it?

612
00:20:25,759 --> 00:20:27,756
How much will I invest each month?

613
00:20:27,936 --> 00:20:29,909
What mix of stocks and bonds will

614
00:20:29,909 --> 00:20:30,328
I hold?

615
00:20:30,508 --> 00:20:31,903
And what will I do when the

616
00:20:31,903 --> 00:20:32,679
market crashes?

617
00:20:33,029 --> 00:20:36,662
Professional investors often use a written investment

618
00:20:36,662 --> 00:20:39,472
policy statement for exactly this reason.

619
00:20:39,652 --> 00:20:41,430
Writing it down when you're calm protects

620
00:20:41,430 --> 00:20:43,425
you from decisions made when you're emotional.

621
00:20:43,776 --> 00:20:46,249
Your plan should also include your rules

622
00:20:46,249 --> 00:20:46,992
for changes.

623
00:20:47,172 --> 00:20:49,698
For example, I'll only change my mix

624
00:20:49,698 --> 00:20:51,873
if my goals or timeline change,

625
00:20:51,873 --> 00:20:53,557
not because of the news.

626
00:20:53,737 --> 00:20:55,200
I'll review once a year.

627
00:20:55,380 --> 00:20:58,039
I'll increase contributions with every raise.

628
00:20:58,219 --> 00:21:00,777
Rules like these turn dozens of emotional

629
00:21:00,777 --> 00:21:02,712
decisions into a few calm ones.

630
00:21:03,062 --> 00:21:04,478
What to do instead.

631
00:21:04,658 --> 00:21:06,469
Write a one page plan this week.

632
00:21:06,649 --> 00:21:08,651
Keep it somewhere you'll see it,

633
00:21:08,651 --> 00:21:10,715
and read it before making any big

634
00:21:10,715 --> 00:21:11,966
investment decision.

635
00:21:12,317 --> 00:21:14,046
Mistake number sixteen.

636
00:21:14,226 --> 00:21:15,794
Making it too complicated.

637
00:21:16,144 --> 00:21:19,122
Some beginners think more investments means more

638
00:21:19,122 --> 00:21:19,557
safety.

639
00:21:19,737 --> 00:21:21,961
So they buy ten or fifteen funds,

640
00:21:21,961 --> 00:21:24,319
a handful of stocks and accounts at

641
00:21:24,319 --> 00:21:25,600
several brokerages.

642
00:21:25,780 --> 00:21:27,982
But many of those funds often hold

643
00:21:27,982 --> 00:21:29,212
the same companies,

644
00:21:29,212 --> 00:21:31,803
so they add complexity without much real

645
00:21:31,803 --> 00:21:32,839
diversification.

646
00:21:33,189 --> 00:21:35,343
Complexity makes it harder to know what

647
00:21:35,343 --> 00:21:37,497
you own, harder to rebalance and easier

648
00:21:37,497 --> 00:21:38,436
to make mistakes.

649
00:21:38,616 --> 00:21:40,447
It also makes it easier to lose

650
00:21:40,447 --> 00:21:42,041
track of accounts and fees.

651
00:21:42,392 --> 00:21:45,420
Many successful investors use just two to

652
00:21:45,420 --> 00:21:46,676
four broad funds.

653
00:21:46,856 --> 00:21:49,052
Some use a single target date fund

654
00:21:49,052 --> 00:21:51,636
that holds a diversified mix and adjusts

655
00:21:51,636 --> 00:21:53,380
it automatically over time.

656
00:21:53,731 --> 00:21:55,066
What to do instead.

657
00:21:55,246 --> 00:21:57,183
Check the top holdings of each fund

658
00:21:57,183 --> 00:21:57,626
you own.

659
00:21:57,806 --> 00:22:00,196
If several look nearly identical,

660
00:22:00,196 --> 00:22:00,848
simplify.

661
00:22:01,028 --> 00:22:03,646
A portfolio you understand is a portfolio

662
00:22:03,646 --> 00:22:04,859
you can stick with.

663
00:22:05,209 --> 00:22:06,927
Mistake number seventeen.

664
00:22:07,107 --> 00:22:09,848
Using margin, leverage or options as a

665
00:22:09,848 --> 00:22:10,497
beginner.

666
00:22:10,847 --> 00:22:13,167
Many trading apps make it easy to

667
00:22:13,167 --> 00:22:14,785
borrow money to invest,

668
00:22:14,785 --> 00:22:16,543
called trading on margin,

669
00:22:16,543 --> 00:22:19,357
or to trade options and leveraged funds.

670
00:22:19,537 --> 00:22:21,493
These tools can multiply gains,

671
00:22:21,493 --> 00:22:23,387
but they also multiply losses,

672
00:22:23,387 --> 00:22:25,469
sometimes beyond what you put in.

673
00:22:25,820 --> 00:22:28,328
With margin, if your investments fall far

674
00:22:28,328 --> 00:22:30,837
enough, your broker can demand more money

675
00:22:30,837 --> 00:22:32,183
or sell your holdings,

676
00:22:32,183 --> 00:22:34,202
often at the worst possible time.

677
00:22:34,382 --> 00:22:36,798
Beginners can lose much more than they

678
00:22:36,798 --> 00:22:38,260
expected, very quickly.

679
00:22:38,610 --> 00:22:41,233
Leveraged ETFs are designed for short term

680
00:22:41,233 --> 00:22:41,733
trading.

681
00:22:41,913 --> 00:22:43,514
Because they reset daily,

682
00:22:43,514 --> 00:22:46,204
their long term results can differ sharply

683
00:22:46,204 --> 00:22:47,741
from what people expect.

684
00:22:47,921 --> 00:22:51,062
The SEC has published investor alerts about

685
00:22:51,062 --> 00:22:51,427
them.

686
00:22:51,777 --> 00:22:53,530
Options deserve their own warning.

687
00:22:53,710 --> 00:22:56,651
They can be useful tools for experienced

688
00:22:56,651 --> 00:22:59,666
investors, but many beginners use them as

689
00:22:59,666 --> 00:23:03,269
lottery tickets, buying short term contracts that

690
00:23:03,269 --> 00:23:04,960
often expire worthless.

691
00:23:05,140 --> 00:23:07,956
Without a deep understanding of how options

692
00:23:07,956 --> 00:23:10,182
are priced, it's very easy to lose

693
00:23:10,182 --> 00:23:12,278
your entire stake in a matter of

694
00:23:12,278 --> 00:23:12,605
days.

695
00:23:12,955 --> 00:23:14,127
What to do instead.

696
00:23:14,307 --> 00:23:15,557
Stick with simple,

697
00:23:15,557 --> 00:23:18,819
unleveraged investments until you have years of

698
00:23:18,819 --> 00:23:22,290
experience, and understand exactly how any complex

699
00:23:22,290 --> 00:23:24,373
product works before using it.

700
00:23:24,723 --> 00:23:26,441
Mistake number eighteen.

701
00:23:26,621 --> 00:23:27,851
Never rebalancing.

702
00:23:28,202 --> 00:23:31,007
Say you start with seventy percent stocks

703
00:23:31,007 --> 00:23:32,718
and thirty percent bonds.

704
00:23:32,898 --> 00:23:35,141
After a few strong years for stocks,

705
00:23:35,141 --> 00:23:37,509
you might drift to eighty five percent

706
00:23:37,509 --> 00:23:39,005
stocks without noticing.

707
00:23:39,185 --> 00:23:41,621
Your portfolio is now riskier than you

708
00:23:41,621 --> 00:23:42,134
planned.

709
00:23:42,484 --> 00:23:44,971
Rebalancing means bringing your mix back to

710
00:23:44,971 --> 00:23:45,665
your target.

711
00:23:45,845 --> 00:23:47,889
You trim what grew and add to

712
00:23:47,889 --> 00:23:48,736
what lagged.

713
00:23:48,916 --> 00:23:50,661
It forces you to sell a bit

714
00:23:50,661 --> 00:23:52,148
high and buy a bit low,

715
00:23:52,148 --> 00:23:54,864
the opposite of what emotions usually push

716
00:23:54,864 --> 00:23:55,510
you to do.

717
00:23:55,860 --> 00:23:58,414
Target date funds rebalance automatically.

718
00:23:58,594 --> 00:24:00,348
If you hold separate funds,

719
00:24:00,348 --> 00:24:02,493
a yearly check is plenty for most

720
00:24:02,493 --> 00:24:02,947
people.

721
00:24:03,298 --> 00:24:04,609
What to do instead.

722
00:24:04,789 --> 00:24:06,379
Pick a date each year to check

723
00:24:06,379 --> 00:24:06,856
your mix.

724
00:24:07,036 --> 00:24:09,132
If it has drifted more than about

725
00:24:09,132 --> 00:24:10,592
five percentage points,

726
00:24:10,592 --> 00:24:13,831
rebalance, ideally using new contributions to avoid

727
00:24:13,831 --> 00:24:14,339
selling.

728
00:24:14,689 --> 00:24:16,244
Mistake number nineteen.

729
00:24:16,424 --> 00:24:19,037
Forgetting old accounts and beneficiaries.

730
00:24:19,387 --> 00:24:21,947
People change jobs many times over a

731
00:24:21,947 --> 00:24:25,077
career, and old retirement accounts get left

732
00:24:25,077 --> 00:24:25,575
behind.

733
00:24:25,755 --> 00:24:28,420
Some studies estimate there are millions of

734
00:24:28,420 --> 00:24:30,589
forgotten four oh one k accounts in

735
00:24:30,589 --> 00:24:31,705
the United States,

736
00:24:31,705 --> 00:24:33,626
holding large amounts of money.

737
00:24:33,806 --> 00:24:36,060
Old accounts can carry higher fees and

738
00:24:36,060 --> 00:24:37,603
are easy to lose track of.

739
00:24:37,953 --> 00:24:41,006
Another overlooked detail is beneficiaries.

740
00:24:41,186 --> 00:24:44,160
Retirement accounts usually pass to whoever is

741
00:24:44,160 --> 00:24:46,487
named as beneficiary on the account,

742
00:24:46,487 --> 00:24:48,814
which can override what a will says.

743
00:24:48,994 --> 00:24:51,505
An outdated beneficiary can send money to

744
00:24:51,505 --> 00:24:52,546
the wrong person.

745
00:24:52,897 --> 00:24:54,570
Neither of these is exciting,

746
00:24:54,570 --> 00:24:56,763
which is exactly why they get ignored.

747
00:24:56,943 --> 00:24:59,546
But a single afternoon of paperwork can

748
00:24:59,546 --> 00:25:01,215
protect years of savings.

749
00:25:01,566 --> 00:25:03,207
If you think you might have lost

750
00:25:03,207 --> 00:25:04,438
track of an old account,

751
00:25:04,438 --> 00:25:05,617
there are ways to look.

752
00:25:05,797 --> 00:25:08,900
Contact former employers' benefits departments,

753
00:25:08,900 --> 00:25:10,287
check old statements,

754
00:25:10,287 --> 00:25:13,390
and search the Department of Labor's retirement

755
00:25:13,390 --> 00:25:15,503
savings lost and found database,

756
00:25:15,503 --> 00:25:18,672
created under recent federal legislation to help

757
00:25:18,672 --> 00:25:20,124
people find old plans.

758
00:25:20,475 --> 00:25:21,740
What to do instead.

759
00:25:21,920 --> 00:25:24,033
List every account you've ever had.

760
00:25:24,213 --> 00:25:27,437
Consider rolling old workplace plans into your

761
00:25:27,437 --> 00:25:29,049
current plan or an IRA,

762
00:25:29,049 --> 00:25:31,852
and check the beneficiaries on each one.

763
00:25:32,202 --> 00:25:33,538
Mistake number twenty.

764
00:25:33,718 --> 00:25:35,761
Never increasing what you invest.

765
00:25:36,110 --> 00:25:38,948
Many beginners start investing a small amount,

766
00:25:38,948 --> 00:25:39,874
which is great,

767
00:25:39,874 --> 00:25:41,416
and then never change it.

768
00:25:41,596 --> 00:25:42,560
Ten years later,

769
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:43,765
they earn much more,

770
00:25:43,765 --> 00:25:46,356
but they're still investing the same amount

771
00:25:46,356 --> 00:25:47,622
as when they started.

772
00:25:47,971 --> 00:25:51,422
Economists Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi designed

773
00:25:51,422 --> 00:25:52,636
Save More Tomorrow,

774
00:25:52,636 --> 00:25:55,511
where employees agreed in advance to increase

775
00:25:55,511 --> 00:25:57,747
their savings rate with each raise.

776
00:25:57,927 --> 00:26:00,774
Participants in their study ended up saving

777
00:26:00,774 --> 00:26:02,165
far more than before.

778
00:26:02,514 --> 00:26:05,462
Increasing contributions over time can matter as

779
00:26:05,462 --> 00:26:07,182
much as the return you earn.

780
00:26:07,362 --> 00:26:09,904
Your investing should grow as your income

781
00:26:09,904 --> 00:26:10,276
grows.

782
00:26:10,625 --> 00:26:11,845
What to do instead.

783
00:26:12,025 --> 00:26:14,940
Turn on automatic increases in your retirement

784
00:26:14,940 --> 00:26:16,398
plan if it offers them,

785
00:26:16,398 --> 00:26:18,490
or set a yearly reminder to raise

786
00:26:18,490 --> 00:26:21,089
your contributions by one percent of your

787
00:26:21,089 --> 00:26:21,533
income.

788
00:26:21,883 --> 00:26:24,345
Why are these mistakes so common now?

789
00:26:24,525 --> 00:26:26,559
Part of the reason is that investing

790
00:26:26,559 --> 00:26:28,480
apps have made trading feel like a

791
00:26:28,480 --> 00:26:28,762
game.

792
00:26:28,942 --> 00:26:33,017
Colorful animations, constant notifications and one tap

793
00:26:33,017 --> 00:26:35,165
trades make it easy to act on

794
00:26:35,165 --> 00:26:35,758
impulse.

795
00:26:35,938 --> 00:26:38,724
Regulators have raised concerns about these game

796
00:26:38,724 --> 00:26:41,046
like features and how they can encourage

797
00:26:41,046 --> 00:26:42,033
frequent trading.

798
00:26:42,382 --> 00:26:45,215
Another reason is the flood of information.

799
00:26:45,395 --> 00:26:48,758
Videos, posts, chat groups and online tools

800
00:26:48,758 --> 00:26:50,243
all offer opinions,

801
00:26:50,243 --> 00:26:51,885
predictions and tips.

802
00:26:52,065 --> 00:26:55,920
More information doesn't automatically mean better decisions.

803
00:26:56,100 --> 00:26:57,802
Often it means more noise,

804
00:26:57,802 --> 00:27:00,094
more temptation and more confusion.

805
00:27:00,443 --> 00:27:02,557
And scammers have new tools too.

806
00:27:02,737 --> 00:27:04,462
Fake investment platforms,

807
00:27:04,462 --> 00:27:07,515
impersonations of well known investors and too

808
00:27:07,515 --> 00:27:09,772
good to be true offers are common.

809
00:27:09,952 --> 00:27:12,309
The basic rules still protect you.

810
00:27:12,489 --> 00:27:14,824
Never send money to someone you only

811
00:27:14,824 --> 00:27:18,197
know online, verify every platform and professional,

812
00:27:18,197 --> 00:27:20,662
and walk away from guaranteed returns.

813
00:27:21,012 --> 00:27:23,319
Let's turn all twenty mistakes into a

814
00:27:23,319 --> 00:27:24,379
simple checklist.

815
00:27:24,559 --> 00:27:26,135
If you can tick these boxes,

816
00:27:26,135 --> 00:27:27,880
you're ahead of most beginners.

817
00:27:28,229 --> 00:27:28,973
Box one.

818
00:27:29,153 --> 00:27:31,117
I have a starter emergency fund,

819
00:27:31,117 --> 00:27:33,328
and I'm building toward three to six

820
00:27:33,328 --> 00:27:35,108
months of essential expenses.

821
00:27:35,288 --> 00:27:36,194
Box two.

822
00:27:36,374 --> 00:27:38,175
I have no credit card debt,

823
00:27:38,175 --> 00:27:39,842
or I have a clear plan to

824
00:27:39,842 --> 00:27:40,575
pay it off,

825
00:27:40,575 --> 00:27:41,842
highest rate first.

826
00:27:42,022 --> 00:27:44,775
These two boxes protect everything else you

827
00:27:44,775 --> 00:27:47,144
build, because they stop a single bad

828
00:27:47,144 --> 00:27:49,257
month from forcing you to sell or

829
00:27:49,257 --> 00:27:50,858
borrow at the worst time.

830
00:27:51,207 --> 00:27:52,206
Box three.

831
00:27:52,386 --> 00:27:54,236
I contribute at least enough to get

832
00:27:54,236 --> 00:27:55,451
my full employer match.

833
00:27:55,631 --> 00:27:56,455
Box four.

834
00:27:56,635 --> 00:27:58,755
I use tax advantaged accounts,

835
00:27:58,755 --> 00:28:00,380
like a four oh one k or

836
00:28:00,380 --> 00:28:03,207
an IRA, before a regular taxable account

837
00:28:03,207 --> 00:28:04,832
where that makes sense.

838
00:28:05,012 --> 00:28:07,108
These two boxes are the closest thing

839
00:28:07,108 --> 00:28:08,694
investing has to free money,

840
00:28:08,694 --> 00:28:10,449
and skipping them is one of the

841
00:28:10,449 --> 00:28:12,942
most common and expensive beginner mistakes.

842
00:28:13,291 --> 00:28:14,093
Box five.

843
00:28:14,273 --> 00:28:15,897
My money is in broad,

844
00:28:15,897 --> 00:28:17,057
low cost funds,

845
00:28:17,057 --> 00:28:19,455
and I know the expense ratio of

846
00:28:19,455 --> 00:28:20,228
every one.

847
00:28:20,408 --> 00:28:21,477
Box six.

848
00:28:21,657 --> 00:28:23,378
I own two to four funds,

849
00:28:23,378 --> 00:28:25,458
or a single target date fund,

850
00:28:25,458 --> 00:28:28,112
and I understand what each one holds.

851
00:28:28,292 --> 00:28:30,939
Simple and cheap is not a compromise.

852
00:28:31,119 --> 00:28:32,197
For most people,

853
00:28:32,197 --> 00:28:34,759
it's the strategy that works best over

854
00:28:34,759 --> 00:28:35,298
decades.

855
00:28:35,648 --> 00:28:36,473
Box seven.

856
00:28:36,653 --> 00:28:38,554
My contributions are automatic,

857
00:28:38,554 --> 00:28:40,333
and they increase every year.

858
00:28:40,513 --> 00:28:41,361
Box eight.

859
00:28:41,541 --> 00:28:43,623
I have a one page written plan,

860
00:28:43,623 --> 00:28:45,906
including what I'll do in a crash.

861
00:28:46,086 --> 00:28:46,934
Box nine.

862
00:28:47,114 --> 00:28:49,490
I review my portfolio once or twice

863
00:28:49,490 --> 00:28:51,595
a year and rebalance if needed.

864
00:28:51,775 --> 00:28:52,530
Box ten.

865
00:28:52,710 --> 00:28:54,714
My beneficiaries are up to date,

866
00:28:54,714 --> 00:28:56,843
and I know where every account is.

867
00:28:57,192 --> 00:28:58,156
Ten boxes.

868
00:28:58,336 --> 00:29:01,224
None of them require predicting the market,

869
00:29:01,224 --> 00:29:03,642
picking stocks or watching the news.

870
00:29:03,822 --> 00:29:05,888
That's the secret most beginners miss.

871
00:29:06,068 --> 00:29:09,064
Successful investing is mostly about building a

872
00:29:09,064 --> 00:29:11,423
good system and then leaving it alone

873
00:29:11,423 --> 00:29:12,698
long enough to work.

874
00:29:13,047 --> 00:29:14,212
In your twenties,

875
00:29:14,212 --> 00:29:17,020
the biggest mistakes are usually waiting,

876
00:29:17,020 --> 00:29:19,828
chasing hype and carrying expensive debt.

877
00:29:20,008 --> 00:29:21,826
Your greatest asset is time,

878
00:29:21,826 --> 00:29:23,644
so the best move is to start

879
00:29:23,644 --> 00:29:26,566
small, automate and avoid gambling with money

880
00:29:26,566 --> 00:29:27,345
you'll need.

881
00:29:27,525 --> 00:29:30,715
Even modest amounts invested now have decades

882
00:29:30,715 --> 00:29:31,565
to compound.

883
00:29:31,915 --> 00:29:33,805
In your thirties and forties,

884
00:29:33,805 --> 00:29:35,108
the big risks shift.

885
00:29:35,288 --> 00:29:38,956
Lifestyle creep, skipping increases as income rises,

886
00:29:38,956 --> 00:29:41,848
too much employer stock and forgotten old

887
00:29:41,848 --> 00:29:43,682
accounts from job changes.

888
00:29:43,862 --> 00:29:46,461
This is the stage to raise contributions,

889
00:29:46,461 --> 00:29:49,568
consolidate accounts and protect your family with

890
00:29:49,568 --> 00:29:51,850
insurance and updated beneficiaries.

891
00:29:52,199 --> 00:29:53,978
In your fifties and beyond,

892
00:29:53,978 --> 00:29:56,680
the biggest mistakes are often taking too

893
00:29:56,680 --> 00:29:58,920
much risk too close to needing the

894
00:29:58,920 --> 00:30:02,083
money, or panicking and selling everything after

895
00:30:02,083 --> 00:30:02,544
a drop.

896
00:30:02,724 --> 00:30:04,684
A thoughtful mix of stocks,

897
00:30:04,684 --> 00:30:05,773
bonds and cash,

898
00:30:05,773 --> 00:30:08,677
plus catch up contributions after fifty,

899
00:30:08,677 --> 00:30:09,330
can help.

900
00:30:09,510 --> 00:30:11,774
Check current limits with the IRS.

901
00:30:12,123 --> 00:30:15,033
Imagine two beginners who both start investing

902
00:30:15,033 --> 00:30:16,930
three hundred dollars a month.

903
00:30:17,110 --> 00:30:19,769
The first one downloads a trading app,

904
00:30:19,769 --> 00:30:21,588
buys whatever is trending,

905
00:30:21,588 --> 00:30:24,736
checks constantly, sells during the first big

906
00:30:24,736 --> 00:30:27,185
drop and jumps back in after prices

907
00:30:27,185 --> 00:30:27,745
recover.

908
00:30:27,925 --> 00:30:29,003
After a few years,

909
00:30:29,003 --> 00:30:30,621
they've paid more in taxes,

910
00:30:30,621 --> 00:30:33,137
missed the rebound and feel that investing

911
00:30:33,137 --> 00:30:33,915
doesn't work.

912
00:30:34,265 --> 00:30:37,197
The second one opens a retirement account,

913
00:30:37,197 --> 00:30:39,222
gets the full employer match,

914
00:30:39,222 --> 00:30:41,735
picks one low cost target date fund,

915
00:30:41,735 --> 00:30:45,086
automates contributions and checks twice a year.

916
00:30:45,266 --> 00:30:46,784
During the same drop,

917
00:30:46,784 --> 00:30:48,229
they keep investing,

918
00:30:48,229 --> 00:30:50,398
buying shares at lower prices.

919
00:30:50,578 --> 00:30:51,874
Their plan is boring,

920
00:30:51,874 --> 00:30:52,923
and it's working.

921
00:30:53,272 --> 00:30:54,306
Same income.

922
00:30:54,486 --> 00:30:55,298
Same market.

923
00:30:55,478 --> 00:30:56,790
Same starting amount.

924
00:30:56,970 --> 00:30:59,269
The difference wasn't intelligence or luck.

925
00:30:59,449 --> 00:31:01,717
It was avoiding a handful of basic

926
00:31:01,717 --> 00:31:02,317
mistakes.

927
00:31:02,497 --> 00:31:05,005
That's the real secret of beginner investing.

928
00:31:05,354 --> 00:31:06,121
Question one.

929
00:31:06,301 --> 00:31:08,286
What's the single biggest mistake?

930
00:31:08,466 --> 00:31:09,530
For most people,

931
00:31:09,530 --> 00:31:10,726
it's not starting,

932
00:31:10,726 --> 00:31:11,856
or starting late.

933
00:31:12,036 --> 00:31:14,156
Time in the market is the ingredient

934
00:31:14,156 --> 00:31:15,275
you can't buy back.

935
00:31:15,455 --> 00:31:17,673
Many other mistakes can be fixed later.

936
00:31:17,853 --> 00:31:18,979
Lost years can't.

937
00:31:19,328 --> 00:31:20,176
Question two.

938
00:31:20,356 --> 00:31:22,469
I've already made some of these mistakes.

939
00:31:22,649 --> 00:31:23,659
Is it too late?

940
00:31:23,839 --> 00:31:24,361
Not at all.

941
00:31:24,541 --> 00:31:27,045
Almost every investor has made some of

942
00:31:27,045 --> 00:31:27,374
them.

943
00:31:27,554 --> 00:31:29,271
The best time to fix them is

944
00:31:29,271 --> 00:31:29,516
now.

945
00:31:29,696 --> 00:31:31,341
Simplify, cut fees,

946
00:31:31,341 --> 00:31:33,504
automate, and keep going.

947
00:31:33,854 --> 00:31:34,911
Question three.

948
00:31:35,091 --> 00:31:36,960
Do I need a financial advisor?

949
00:31:37,140 --> 00:31:38,417
Not necessarily.

950
00:31:38,597 --> 00:31:40,901
Many people do well with a simple,

951
00:31:40,901 --> 00:31:42,730
low cost plan on their own.

952
00:31:42,910 --> 00:31:43,973
If you want help,

953
00:31:43,973 --> 00:31:45,848
look for a fee only fiduciary,

954
00:31:45,848 --> 00:31:47,973
who's required to act in your best

955
00:31:47,973 --> 00:31:51,223
interest, and understand exactly what you're paying.

956
00:31:51,573 --> 00:31:52,398
Question four.

957
00:31:52,578 --> 00:31:54,369
Should I invest a lump sum all

958
00:31:54,369 --> 00:31:55,921
at once, or spread it out?

959
00:31:56,101 --> 00:31:58,771
Historically, investing a lump sum right away

960
00:31:58,771 --> 00:32:00,254
has often come out ahead,

961
00:32:00,254 --> 00:32:02,746
because markets have risen more often than

962
00:32:02,746 --> 00:32:03,636
they've fallen.

963
00:32:03,816 --> 00:32:06,109
But spreading it over several months can

964
00:32:06,109 --> 00:32:07,485
feel easier emotionally.

965
00:32:07,665 --> 00:32:10,070
Either is far better than leaving money

966
00:32:10,070 --> 00:32:12,414
uninvested for years while you decide.

967
00:32:12,763 --> 00:32:13,715
Question five.

968
00:32:13,895 --> 00:32:15,456
How much should I have in stocks

969
00:32:15,456 --> 00:32:16,090
versus bonds?

970
00:32:16,270 --> 00:32:18,330
It depends on your timeline and how

971
00:32:18,330 --> 00:32:19,625
well you handle drops.

972
00:32:19,805 --> 00:32:21,988
A common starting point is to hold

973
00:32:21,988 --> 00:32:24,363
more stocks when your goal is decades

974
00:32:24,363 --> 00:32:26,546
away and gradually add bonds as it

975
00:32:26,546 --> 00:32:27,317
gets closer.

976
00:32:27,497 --> 00:32:29,078
Target date funds do this for you

977
00:32:29,078 --> 00:32:29,749
automatically.

978
00:32:30,098 --> 00:32:31,306
Question six.

979
00:32:31,486 --> 00:32:33,070
Is it okay to invest if I

980
00:32:33,070 --> 00:32:35,097
don't understand everything yet?

981
00:32:35,277 --> 00:32:35,671
Yes.

982
00:32:35,851 --> 00:32:38,613
You don't need to understand every detail

983
00:32:38,613 --> 00:32:40,163
to start with a simple,

984
00:32:40,163 --> 00:32:41,981
diversified, low cost fund.

985
00:32:42,161 --> 00:32:44,046
You can keep learning while your money

986
00:32:44,046 --> 00:32:44,344
works.

987
00:32:44,524 --> 00:32:47,706
Just avoid anything complex you can't explain

988
00:32:47,706 --> 00:32:49,191
in a sentence or two.

989
00:32:49,541 --> 00:32:50,366
Quick recap.

990
00:32:50,546 --> 00:32:52,299
Angle one, the numbers.

991
00:32:52,479 --> 00:32:55,337
Waiting, fees, skipping the match,

992
00:32:55,337 --> 00:32:58,196
expensive debt, no emergency fund,

993
00:32:58,196 --> 00:33:01,895
expecting smooth returns and ignoring taxes.

994
00:33:02,244 --> 00:33:04,462
Angle two, the psychology.

995
00:33:04,642 --> 00:33:07,037
Panic selling, chasing winners,

996
00:33:07,037 --> 00:33:10,205
hype, overconfidence, checking too often,

997
00:33:10,205 --> 00:33:12,910
social media advice and too much in

998
00:33:12,910 --> 00:33:13,837
one company.

999
00:33:14,187 --> 00:33:15,894
Angle three, the playbook.

1000
00:33:16,074 --> 00:33:18,103
No plan, too much complexity,

1001
00:33:18,103 --> 00:33:19,432
leverage too early,

1002
00:33:19,432 --> 00:33:23,489
never rebalancing, forgotten accounts and never increasing

1003
00:33:23,489 --> 00:33:24,468
contributions.

1004
00:33:24,818 --> 00:33:26,666
You don't need to be brilliant to

1005
00:33:26,666 --> 00:33:27,338
invest well.

1006
00:33:27,518 --> 00:33:29,465
You just need to avoid the big

1007
00:33:29,465 --> 00:33:31,802
mistakes, keep it simple and give it

1008
00:33:31,802 --> 00:33:32,127
time.

1009
00:33:32,307 --> 00:33:33,871
If you want my full step by

1010
00:33:33,871 --> 00:33:35,666
step plan, it's in my playbook,

1011
00:33:35,666 --> 00:33:37,172
linked in the description.

1012
00:33:37,352 --> 00:33:38,637
Tell me in the comments,

1013
00:33:38,637 --> 00:33:40,672
which of these mistakes have you made?

1014
00:33:40,852 --> 00:33:42,118
I'm Ryan Rover.

1015
00:33:42,298 --> 00:33:43,505
See you in the next one.
